34:["$","section","03-dns-step",{"id":"03-dns-step","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"03"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"Step 1: DNS turns the name into an IP"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Machines route by IP address, not by human names. So before anything can be sent, the hostname api.example.com must be resolved to an IP, here, 10.0.0.1. That resolution is done by DNS (the Domain Name System)."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Under the hood this walks a chain of servers, a recursive resolver asks the root servers, then the TLD servers (.com), then the authoritative server for example.com, until it gets the answer. We will dedicate a whole chapter to how DNS resolution works."}],["$","div","2",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["Analogy",": "]}],"DNS is the internet’s phone book: you know the name, DNS gives you the number."]}]]}]]}],["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L2c",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/cn-notes/objects/ch01%2F03-dns-step.jpg?v=731667c3b64790dc85909203e6a1bd5e","alt":"api.example.com resolved via DNS to 10.0.0.1 through root, TLD and authoritative servers."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]]}]]}]
35:["$","section","04-http-request",{"id":"04-http-request","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"04"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"Step 2: Build the HTTP request"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"With the destination known, the browser assembles the actual HTTP request. This is just structured text: a method, a path, and a set of headers."}],["$","dl","1",{"className":"flex flex-col gap-2.5","children":[["$","div","0",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Method"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"GET, POST, PUT, etc., what you want to do."}]]}],["$","div","1",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Path"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"/profile, the resource on the server."}]]}],["$","div","2",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Headers"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Metadata: content type, host, user agent, and more."}]]}],["$","div","3",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Auth & cookies"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"An Authorization: Bearer <token> header and cookies often travel with the request."}]]}]]}]]}],["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L2c",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/cn-notes/objects/ch01%2F04-http-request.jpg?v=81b107d4385544668c9671e3bde97c5f","alt":"HTTP request document showing method, path, headers, bearer token and cookies."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]]}]]}]
36:["$","section","05-http-on-tcp",{"id":"05-http-on-tcp","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"05"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"Step 3: HTTP rides on TCP"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"HTTP does not move data by itself, it is carried by TCP (Transmission Control Protocol). TCP is connection-oriented: before any data flows, the client and server establish a reliable \"pipe\" between them."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"TCP is what makes the delivery reliable and ordered, using mechanisms like the three-way handshake, flow control, and congestion control, each a future deep-dive. For now: HTTP text is handed down to TCP to actually transport."}],["$","div","2",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["Mental model",": "]}],"HTTP = what you want to say. TCP = the reliable courier that guarantees it arrives."]}]]}]]}],["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L2c",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/cn-notes/objects/ch01%2F05-http-on-tcp.jpg?v=a7c973a6ab0a2a36c28a71768b02474d","alt":"HTTP layered on top of TCP, with a client-server connection pipe."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]]}]]}]
37:["$","section","06-text-to-bytes",{"id":"06-text-to-bytes","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"06"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"Step 4: Text becomes bytes"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"The network does not move text, it moves bytes. The HTTP request text is run through an encoder that turns each character into its byte value, which we often view in hexadecimal (e.g. FE 12 00 AA …)."}],["$","div","1",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["Why it matters",": "]}],"Everything below this point is just bytes. The \"meaning\" (it was a GET request) is only reconstructed when the server decodes them at the other end."]}]]}]]}],["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L2c",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/cn-notes/objects/ch01%2F06-text-to-bytes.jpg?v=bd3656f8d9e18f22b37c769f866139ac","alt":"HTTP text encoded into hexadecimal bytes."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]]}]]}]
38:["$","section","07-os-stack",{"id":"07-os-stack","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"07"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"Step 5: The browser hands off to the OS"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"The browser prepares the request (DNS, IP, ports) but it does not transmit anything itself. It hands the bytes to the operating system’s TCP/IP stack, which is the part of the OS (macOS, Windows, Linux) that actually implements TCP, UDP, ICMP and IP and talks to the hardware."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"The OS stack then drives the NIC (Network Interface Card), the physical hardware that puts signals on the wire or the air."}],["$","div","2",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["Key split",": "]}],"App/browser = prepares the message. OS network stack = transmits it. Different responsibilities, different layers."]}]]}]]}],["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L2c",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/cn-notes/objects/ch01%2F07-os-stack.jpg?v=efe22c07f72f8b4fe27e2f0f05efff9b","alt":"Browser hands to the OS TCP/IP stack (TCP/UDP/ICMP/IP) which drives the NIC."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]]}]]}]
39:["$","section","08-segmentation",{"id":"08-segmentation","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"08"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"Step 6: Break the data into chunks"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Data cannot travel as one giant blob. A 500-byte request or a 1 GB file is broken into many smaller chunks that are sent independently and reassembled at the destination."}],["$","dl","1",{"className":"flex flex-col gap-2.5","children":[["$","div","0",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"MTU"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Maximum Transmission Unit, the largest frame size the link can carry."}]]}],["$","div","1",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"MSS"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Maximum Segment Size, the largest chunk of data a single TCP segment can hold."}]]}]]}],["$","div","2",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["Later",": "]}],"MTU, MSS, PMTUD and IP fragmentation get a dedicated chapter. For now: chunk sizes are capped by these limits."]}]]}]]}],["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L2c",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/cn-notes/objects/ch01%2F08-segmentation.jpg?v=c1956e4be701ab092ef7ae478f59f308","alt":"A block of data split into numbered chunks, limited by MTU and MSS."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]]}]]}]
3a:["$","section","09-tcp-segment",{"id":"09-tcp-segment","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"09"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"Wrap 1: The TCP segment"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Each chunk is wrapped by TCP into a TCP segment. TCP adds the source port (3000), the destination port (443), and, crucially, a sequence number."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"The sequence number lets the receiver put chunks back in the right order even if they arrive out of order, and detect if any are missing. This is the heart of TCP’s reliability."}],["$","div","2",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["First envelope",": "]}],"Chunk + ports + sequence number = TCP segment. It knows which programs to connect, but not yet which machines."]}]]}]]}],["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L2c",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/cn-notes/objects/ch01%2F09-tcp-segment.jpg?v=a25e53beb1145602c0178b7bde6f9e77","alt":"A data chunk wrapped with source port, destination port and a sequence number to form a TCP segment."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]]}]]}]
3b:["$","section","10-ip-packet",{"id":"10-ip-packet","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"10"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"Wrap 2: The IP packet"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"The TCP segment is then wrapped by the IP layer into an IP packet, which adds the source IP (127.0.0.1) and destination IP (10.0.0.1)."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Routers along the way read the destination IP to decide where to forward the packet next, hop by hop. The source IP is what lets the backend know where to send the response."}],["$","div","2",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["Second envelope",": "]}],"TCP segment + source/dest IP = IP packet. Now it knows which machines, this is what the internet routes on."]}]]}]]}],["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L2c",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/cn-notes/objects/ch01%2F10-ip-packet.jpg?v=962001dbdaed222e07433d3f3142eca7","alt":"A TCP segment wrapped with source and destination IP addresses to form an IP packet."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]]}]]}]
3c:["$","section","11-frame-mac",{"id":"11-frame-mac","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"11"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"Wrap 3: The frame (MAC addresses)"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"The final wrap adds physical (MAC) addresses, producing a frame. The source MAC is your laptop’s NIC. But what is the destination MAC?"}],["$","p","1",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"The backend is not on your local network, so you do not know (and cannot use) its MAC directly. Instead, the destination MAC is set to your default gateway (the router), the device that will forward the frame onward toward the destination."}],["$","div","2",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["Third envelope",": "]}],"IP packet + source/dest MAC = frame. MAC gets it to the next physical device, here, the router."]}]]}]]}],["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L2c",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/cn-notes/objects/ch01%2F11-frame-mac.jpg?v=4bc0a1dda6cfa757f9bd165082c077b3","alt":"An IP packet wrapped with source and destination MAC addresses to form a frame; destination MAC is the default gateway."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]]}]]}]
3d:["$","section","12-arp-and-wire",{"id":"12-arp-and-wire","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"12"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"Step 7: ARP, then onto the wire"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"But how do we learn the router’s MAC address? With ARP (Address Resolution Protocol). Your laptop broadcasts to the whole local network: \"who has 192.168.1.1?\", and only the router replies with its MAC. ARP is like DNS, but it maps an IP to a MAC."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Finally the completed frame is handed to the NIC, which transmits it as physical signals: radio waves (WiFi), electrical pulses (Ethernet), or light (fiber)."}],["$","div","2",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["Reality check",": "]}],"After all this, the data still has not reached the backend, we have only covered roughly the first quarter of the journey. Everything above gets unpacked, routed, and repeated at every hop. That is what the rest of the course explores."]}]]}]]}],["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L2c",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/cn-notes/objects/ch01%2F12-arp-and-wire.jpg?v=400a7da804ab10fc0b9ac6a8f58a475b","alt":"ARP broadcast asking who has the gateway IP, then the NIC emitting radio, electric and light signals."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]]}]]}]
